    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi, i am studying english phonetics and i am a bit confused. can you explain the difference between voiced and unvoiced alveolar fricatives? like what is actually happening with the vocal cords for /z/ versus /s/?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Voiced and unvoiced fricatives differ in vocal cord vibration. For the unvoiced /s/, there is no vibration in the vocal cords as the air passes through a narrow opening made by the tongue near the alveolar ridge. In contrast, for the voiced /z/, the vocal cords vibrate as a stream of air passes through the same restricted airflow, creating the characteristic buzzing sound. Essentially, the difference is whether the vocal cords are active or not during the articulation.",
      "source": "target",
